# Line endings.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows and its scripts are executed inside
# Linux containers. A shell script committed with CRLF fails there with
# `/bin/sh: bad interpreter: No such file or directory` -- the carriage return
# becomes part of the interpreter path -- and the error names neither the file
# nor the cause. Postgres init SQL, nginx-style conf files and HCL are the same
# story with quieter failures.
#
# This file governs what is written INTO commits, so it has to exist before the
# first script does. It does not retroactively fix an already-checked-out tree:
# after adding or changing it, run
#     git add --renormalize .
# and commit whatever that stages.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, because `text=auto` guesses from content and a file that begins with
# a UTF-8 BOM or holds a stray 0x00 is guessed to be binary and passed through
# unchanged -- CRLF and all.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
*.hcl       text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.env       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf

# PowerShell runs on Windows and is never fed to a container shell. CRLF is what
# every Windows editor and `Set-Content` produce; forcing LF here creates noisy
# diffs for no gain.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.psm1      text eol=crlf

# Never touched.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.webp      binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.tar       binary
